User Journey
A user types www.foobar.com in their browser.

The DNS system resolves www.foobar.com to the IP of the load balancer.

The load balancer (HAProxy) distributes traffic between two backend servers.

Each backend server runs:

Web server (Nginx)

Application server (executes the codebase)

Application files (codebase)

Database (MySQL) (configured in a Primary‑Replica cluster).

The user receives the website content securely over TCP/IP with HTTPS.

🖥️ Infrastructure Components and Why They’re Added
Load balancer (HAProxy) → Added to distribute traffic between servers, prevent overload, and increase availability.

Two servers → Added for redundancy and scalability; if one fails, the other continues serving requests.

Web server (Nginx) → Handles HTTP requests, serves static files, and forwards dynamic requests.

Application server → Runs business logic and generates dynamic responses.

Codebase → The actual source code of the website.

Database (MySQL) → Stores persistent data, configured in Primary‑Replica mode for reliability.

⚙️ Load Balancer Specifics
Distribution algorithm → Commonly Round Robin: requests are sent alternately to each server, balancing load evenly.

Active‑Active setup → Both servers are actively serving traffic at the same time.

Active‑Passive setup → One server handles traffic, the other stays idle until the first fails.

In our design, we use Active‑Active for better resource utilization and redundancy.

📊 Database Primary‑Replica Cluster
Primary node → Handles all write operations (insert, update, delete).

Replica node(s) → Handle read operations (select queries), synchronized from the Primary.

Difference for the application →

Writes go to the Primary.

Reads can be distributed to Replicas, improving performance and reducing load on the Primary.

⚠️ Issues with This Infrastructure
SPOF (Single Point of Failure) → The load balancer itself is a SPOF; if it fails, the whole system is unreachable.

Security issues → No firewall, no HTTPS termination at the load balancer, leaving traffic vulnerable.

No monitoring → Without monitoring tools, failures or performance issues may go unnoticed.

